        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(gateway): simplify node connection state",
        "body": "Related: #110354\nFollow-up to #110391.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe node-connection notification fix left connection timestamps writable through two paths and tracked each pending alert across two maps plus a generation counter. That made the first-connection invariant harder to audit and allowed a delayed Watch connect completion to overwrite a newer timestamp.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis makes the locked connection recorder the only writer for `lastConnectedAtMs` across WebSocket and Watch transports, narrows the unrelated remote-bin update API, and stores timer ownership directly on each pending alert. Object identity now invalidates replaced or in-flight alert attempts without separate generation bookkeeping.\n\nThe WebSocket handshake ordering and first-connection notification behavior remain unchanged. This is an AI-assisted maintainer-requested follow-up refactor; no changelog entry is needed for the internal-only surface.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo intended visible behavior change. New nodes still alert once, reconnects remain silent, replacement connections retain pending-alert ownership, and stale Watch completions can no longer move the stored connection timestamp backward.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Exact-head isolated live proof used an actual Gateway child process plus authenticated synthetic macOS and Linux node clients. The macOS target received one setup alert; a Gateway restart produced zero known-node alerts; approving and connecting the new Linux node produced exactly one `system.notify`; a second Gateway restart produced zero additional alerts. Blacksmith Testbox `tbx_01kxsxdh2kaepwcxjzj62fkere` passed the complete protocol path ([run](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29633463544)).\n- Watch ordering proof records timestamp `2000`, then a stale `1500`, and verifies the persisted value remains `2000`. WebSocket and Watch now both use that same locked recorder; the Watch path does not schedule connection notifications.\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs` across the notification router, WebSocket event dependency, Watch transport, pairing store/migration, and remote skill-bin suites: 6 files, 111 tests passed.\n- Delegated Blacksmith Testbox `pnpm check:changed`: core and core-test typechecks, lint, formatting, import-cycle, pairing, storage, and repository guards passed. Run: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29632902641\n- Fresh uncommitted autoreview: no accepted/actionable findings.\n",
